# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-autocad-error-primitive-masks-load-failure — deported LOAD calls undefined ERROR and hides the original failure
#+STATUS: NEXT
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: cad-runtime
#+DEPENDS: alfe-autocad-hung-instance-blocks-com-bootstrap
#+BRANCH: fix-alfe-autocad-error-primitive-masks-load-failure
#+DATE: 2026-09-26

* Observed

Two SCHME+ jobs driven by ALFE against AutoCAD 2022 fail on the first
=tu:load= in their test entry point:

#+begin_example
ERROR protocol request 5: while loading .../alfe/esrc-test-cad.lsp
  (form starting at line 5) at line 5, column 1:
  no function definition: ERROR
#+end_example

The unit-test entry point fails identically on its own first =tu:load=:

#+begin_example
ERROR protocol request 5: while loading .../alfe/esrc-test-unitaire.lsp
  (form starting at line 5) at line 5, column 1:
  no function definition: ERROR
#+end_example

The generated entry points are ordinary AutoLISP.  Line 5 is respectively:

#+begin_src lisp
(tu:load "LSP/fonctions-utilitaires/technique/logiciel.lsp")
(tu:load "LSP/fonctions-utilitaires/technique/autolisp.lsp")
#+end_src

Both equivalent BricsCAD jobs pass in the same pipeline: CAD reports 6/6 and
the unit group 20/20.  The failure is therefore on the AutoCAD path through
ALFE's CAD-side runtime, before SCHME+'s assertions execute.

Observed in SCHME+ pipeline 86651 at commit =bf0cd375=:

- job 516229, =test-cad:autocad:windows=;
- job 516232, =test-unitaire:autocad:windows=.

The two identical lines in each job are a separate known reporting defect;
see =issues/open/alfe-cad-error-reported-twice-on-stderr.issue=.

* Cause

AutoCAD reports exactly what the CAD-side runtime asks it to resolve: the
function =ERROR=.  That function is not available in this AutoCAD AutoLISP
host, but the deported source loader uses it as its rethrow primitive in
=autolisp-front-end/source/runtime/autolisp-bootstrap.lsp=.

At least two paths are affected:

#+begin_src lisp
(defun autolisp-source-raise (... / msg)
  ...
  (error msg))

(defun autolisp-source-load-core-impl (... / ... catch-result)
  ...
  (if (vl-catch-all-error-p catch-result)
    (error (vl-catch-all-error-message catch-result))
    catch-result))
#+end_src

The source-aware loader catches an error while evaluating a loaded form and
then tries to re-signal it by calling =ERROR=.  On AutoCAD that attempted
rethrow raises a new undefined-function error.  Consequently the useful
original condition and message are lost; ALFE can only report =no function
definition: ERROR= against the outer =tu:load= form.

This remains present on =master= at =b8a1f15a=, so it is not only a stale
installed-runtime symptom.

* Reproduction

On a Windows host with AutoCAD 2022, run ALFE's source-aware loader on a file
which performs a nested =load= and whose nested evaluation signals an error.
The SCHME+ reproducer is:

#+begin_example
make test-cad-alfe CAD=autocad
make test-unitaire-alfe CAD=autocad
#+end_example

With =--keep-workdir=, inspect =alfe/esrc-test-cad.lsp= or
=alfe/esrc-test-unitaire.lsp= and =console.err=.  The request reaches the
first nested load, then reports the undefined =ERROR= function instead of the
condition which made the source loader rethrow.

A minimal regression scenario should not depend on SCHME+: stage an outer
file which loads an inner file whose evaluated form fails, and exercise it
through the emitted AutoCAD runtime.

* Fix direction

Do not use an assumed =ERROR= function as the CAD-portable non-local signalling
mechanism.  Refactor the source loader so that it transports a structured
failure through the existing =vl-catch-all-apply= / =*error*= boundary, or use
an error-signalling operation whose presence and semantics have been verified
on every supported CAD backend.

The fix must preserve the source-aware diagnostic already assembled by
=autolisp-source-format-error=, including the inner pathname, line, column,
form start and nested load stack.  Merely avoiding the call while returning
=nil= would turn a failed load into an apparent success and is not acceptable.

Audit every =(error ...)= in the emitted CAD runtime, not only the two calls
above: bootstrap, request evaluation, quit propagation and legacy batch paths
contain further calls which can fail the same way once reached under AutoCAD.

* Acceptance criteria

- The emitted AutoCAD runtime contains no reachable dependency on an
  undefined =ERROR= function.
- A nested loaded-file failure reaches ALFE as a failed request with the
  original error text and the correct inner source location/load stack; it is
  not replaced by =no function definition: ERROR=.
- A regression test exercises the emitted runtime with =ERROR= unavailable
  and proves both the request status and diagnostic text.
- The SCHME+ AutoCAD CAD and unit entry points get past their first =tu:load=
  (or, if an independent source error remains, report that underlying error).
- BricsCAD and clautolisp source-load behaviour remains unchanged.

* Progress — clautolisp 2.2.98: the ERROR dependency is gone

The audit asked for is done, and it found FIFTEEN calls, not two: three
diagnostic raises in the source loader and TWELVE
=(error *AUTOLISP_QUIT_SIGNAL*)= in the quit-propagation and batch
paths. Every one of them would have failed the same way once reached
under AutoCAD, so =(quit)= from a loaded file was broken there too. A
sixteenth lived on the alfe side, in the =autolisp-eval-request-form=
override emitted into run-common.lsp (=(if err (error err) r)=, added
with the 2.2.91 fix).

All sixteen now go through one helper, =autolisp-raise= (bootstrap):

- it raises with ARITHMETIC -- =(/ 1 0)=, which fails on every
  supported host and IS trappable by =vl-catch-all-apply=, unlike an
  undefined-function error;
- the message travels where the source loader's diagnostic already
  travelled, in =*AUTOLISP_LAST_ERROR_CONTEXT*=, which
  =autolisp-effective-error-message= already prefers -- so the inner
  pathname, line, column, form start and nested load stack survive
  unchanged;
- a quit is not written into the error context: =autolisp-raise= sets
  =*AUTOLISP_QUIT_REQUESTED*=, which every quit test consults BEFORE the
  message, so quit propagation is preserved without depending on the
  message text;
- =autolisp-force-error= has two further statements after the division
  (=(car 0)=, then an undefined function) so the abort is certain even on
  a host that would tolerate a division by zero. They are unreachable on
  every host we know.

Tests (autolisp-front-end/tests/file-protocol-tests.lisp):

- =protocol-emitted-runtime-never-calls-error= — run-common.lsp AND the
  staged bootstrap and protocol runtime contain no =(error = call at all,
  and the replacement helpers are present. This is acceptance criterion 1
  in mechanical form.
- =protocol-loader-failure-reports-its-diagnostic-without-error= — hosted
  under clautolisp: a loaded file whose form the LOADER rejects reports
  =DONE 1 FAIL= with the file, the line and the original text
  (=LOAD failed: "/nonexistent-file-xyz"=), the session stays usable, and
  =(quit)= still quits rather than failing.

Suites: alfe 1171 checks 0 failures.

** What is NOT done, and why

- *Criterion 3, "with ERROR unavailable", is met mechanically, not by
  running.* clautolisp HAS =error=, and shadowing it with a defun does
  not reproduce an ABSENT function (AutoCAD's symptom is that resolving
  the symbol fails, which no shadow can imitate). The text gate above
  proves the property AutoCAD needs -- no call exists -- and the hosted
  test proves the replacement carries the diagnostic.
- *Criterion 2 is MET as of 2.2.100.* It was blocked by a second defect
  found while testing this one, which looked like the failure being
  swallowed on the override's path. It was not: the CAD-side loader ran
  only the FIRST form of each line of a loaded file and discarded the
  rest in silence, so the form that should have failed had never run.
  Fixed and closed as
  =issues/closed/alfe-cad-source-loader-drops-a-second-form-on-a-line.issue=
  (the re-entrancy write-up that preceded it is kept, marked
  MISDIAGNOSED). With that in place a failure inside a loaded file
  reports =DONE n FAIL= with the engine's message and the inner source
  location, at one nesting level and at two, which is this criterion.
- *Criterion 4 needs SCHME+ and a working AutoCAD.* The Windows runner
  currently cannot start AutoCAD at all -- two wedged acad.exe hold the
  COM registration, see
  =issues/open/alfe-autocad-hung-instance-blocks-com-bootstrap.issue=.
